  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'How do confidence intervals provide more information than point estimates in statistical reporting? (5 marks)', 'A point estimate (e.g. sample mean or proportion) gives a single best guess but says nothing about how precise it is. A confidence interval gives a range of plausible values for the population parameter together with a stated confidence level (e.g. 95%), so it shows the uncertainty due to sampling. Its width shows precision - narrow intervals from large samples or low variability, wide ones from small samples. It also supports inference: if a 95% CI for a difference excludes zero the difference is significant at 5%, and comparing intervals across groups or years shows whether changes are meaningful. It helps judge practical importance, not only statistical significance, and prevents over-interpreting small differences. Example: reporting an unemployment rate of 4.5% (95% CI 4.0-5.0%) tells users the true rate is likely between 4% and 5%, which a bare 4.5% does not.', null, '[{"point": "Point estimate gives a single value with no indication of precision/ uncertainty", "marks": 1}, {"point": "CI gives a range of plausible values with a stated confidence level; shows precision (width), effect of sample size and variability", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Helps practical interpretation and comparison: significance (whether CI includes null value/ overlap), practical importance, honest reporting, example (e.g. unemployment 4.5% with 95% CI 4.0-5.0)", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'statistics-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 5,
