  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What role do assumptions play in the application of statistical models, and how can violations of these assumptions affect the results? (5 marks)', 'Every statistical model rests on assumptions - for example, in linear regression: a linear relationship, independent observations, constant error variance (homoscedasticity), normally distributed errors (for small-sample inference), no perfect multicollinearity, and data from a random sample. These assumptions justify the estimates, their standard errors, p-values and confidence intervals. When they are violated: non-linearity or omitted variables give biased estimates and poor predictions; heteroscedasticity or autocorrelation (common in time series) make standard errors wrong, so tests and intervals are invalid (too many false positives or missed effects); non-normal errors in small samples distort tests; outliers can dominate results; multicollinearity inflates standard errors and makes coefficients unstable; non-random samples bias all results. Therefore assumptions should be checked with diagnostics (residual plots, normality and heteroscedasticity tests, VIF) and handled by transformations, robust standard errors, weighting, non-parametric methods or more appropriate models.', null, '[{"point": "Role: models rely on assumptions (e.g. linearity, independence, normality, constant variance, random sampling, no multicollinearity) that justify estimates, standard errors and tests", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Effects of violations: biased/ inconsistent estimates, wrong standard errors, invalid p-values and CIs (Type I/ II errors), poor predictions, misleading conclusions - with examples (heteroscedasticity, autocorrelation, outliers, non-normal small samples)", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Response: check with diagnostics (residual plots, tests), transform data, robust SEs, non-parametric or other models", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'statistics-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 5,
    540, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
